(2)\nSubject motivated to seek treatment for erectile dysfunction.\nSubject with a total serum testosterone level = 300 ng/dL, with or without supplementation\nHoehn and Yahr Scale score of 1 - 3\nPatient able to consent and comply with protocol requirements\n\nExclusion Criteria:\nSubject unwilling to cease use of any treatment for erectile dysfunction during the study, including oral medication, vacuum devices, constrictive devices, injections, urethral suppositories, gels, any over-the-counter or nonprescription medications, and products purchased via the internet\nSubject receiving dopamine agonists, nitrates, alpha-receptor blocking agents, or antihypertensive medication (see other exclusionary medications listed below)\nSubject with a history of syncope within the last 6 months prior to screening\nSubject with symptomatic postural hypotension (severe dizziness or fainting\nSubject with hypotension and a resting systolic blood pressure of < 90 mmHG or hypertension with a resting systolic blood pressure > 170 mmHG or a resting diastolic blood pressure > 110 mmHG\nSubject with any underlying cardiovascular condition, including unstable angina pectoris, which preclude sexual activity\nSubject with a history of myocardial infarction, stroke or life-threatening arrhythmia within 6 months prior to screening\nSubject with uncontrolled atrial fibrillation/flutter at screening (defined as ventricular response rate = 100 bpm)\nSubject with a bleeding disorder\nSubject with a history of prostatectomy because of prostate cancer, including nerve sparing techniques. Subjects with a history of surgical procedures for the treatment of benign prostate hypertrophy are permitted, with the exception of cryosurgery, cryotherapy or cryoablation\nSubject with hereditary degenerative retinal disorders such as retinitis pigmentosa\nSubject with a history of loss of vision because of non-arteritic anterior ischemic optic neuropathy (NAION), history of temporary or permanent loss of vision, including unilateral loss of vision\nSubject with a history of congenital QT prolongation\nSubject with a penile anatomical abnormality (e.g., penile fibrosis, fractures, or Peyronie\'s disease) which, in the investigator\'s opinion, could significantly impair sexual performance. This will be based on subject\'s reported medical history (penile exam not required)\nSubject with primary hypoactive sexual desire.\nSubject with a spinal cord injury\nSubject with a severe chronic or acute liver disease, history of moderate (Child-Pugh B), or severe (Child-Pugh C) hepatic impairment\nSubject with clinically significant chronic hematological disease which could lead to priapism such as sickle cell anemia, multiple myeloma, and leukemia\nSubject with active peptic ulceration\nSubject with a history of malignancy within the past 5 years (other than squamous or basal cell skin cancer)\nSubject with a history of a positive test for Hepatitis B surface antigen (HbsAg) or Hepatitis C\nSubject with a known hypersensitivity to any component of the investigational medications, monoamine oxidase inhibitors, phosphodiesterase type 5 inhibitors or phenylethylamines\nSubjects with a history of drug or alcohol abuse within the past 6 months\nSubjects currently consuming =5 units of alcohol per day\nSubject who is illiterate or unable to understand the Informed Consent Form, questionnaires or subject diary\nSubject who, in the opinion of the investigator, will be noncompliant with the visit schedule or study procedures\nSubject with any unstable medical, psychiatric, or substance abuse disorder that in the opinion of the investigator is likely to affect the subject\'s ability to complete the study or preclude the subject\'s participation in the study\nDiagnosis of any other neurologic disease\nUncontrolled Diabetes (Hemoglobin A1C > 7.5)\n\n\n### Response:\nInclusion Criteria:\nDiagnosis of idiopathic Parkinson\'s disease that is optimally treated (motor fluctuations <20% of subject\'s awake time). Subjects may be on levodopa therapy [AND] but must be stable at the time of entry into the study\nSexually active (i.e. =1 attempt/week) males, 40 - 64 years of age (inclusive) at time of screening\nDiagnosis of moderate erectile dysfunction (defined according to the NIH Consensus Development Panel on Impotence) for more than 6 months [AND] and demonstrating and incomplete response to tadalafil alone\nSubject demonstrating an IIEF-5 drug-free baseline score that is = 10 [AND] but = 16, and an IIEF-5 tadalafil-alone baseline score that is = 18\nSubject in a stable heterosexual relationship for at least 6 months. (2)\nSubject motivated to seek treatment for erectile dysfunction.\nSubject with a total serum testosterone level = 300 ng/dL, [AND] with or without supplementation\nHoehn and Yahr Scale score of 1 - 3\nPatient able to consent and comply with protocol requirements\n\nExclusion Criteria:\nSubject unwilling to cease use of any treatment for erectile dysfunction during the study, including oral medication, [OR] vacuum devices, [OR] constrictive devices, [OR] injections, [OR] urethral suppositories, [OR] gels, any over-the-counter [OR] or nonprescription medications, and products purchased via the internet\nSubject receiving dopamine agonists, [OR] nitrates, [OR] alpha-receptor blocking agents, [OR] or antihypertensive medication (see other exclusionary medications listed below)\n Subject with a history of syncope within the last 6 months prior to screening\nSubject with symptomatic postural hypotension (severe dizziness [OR] or fainting\nSubject with hypotension [AND] and a resting systolic blood pressure of < 90 mmHG [OR] or hypertension [AND] with a resting systolic blood pressure > 170 mmHG [OR] or a resting diastolic blood pressure > 110 mmHG\n Subject with any underlying cardiovascular condition, including unstable angina pectoris, which [NOT] preclude sexual activity\n Subject with a history of myocardial infarction, [OR] stroke [OR] or life-threatening arrhythmia within 6 months prior to screening\n Subject with uncontrolled atrial fibrillation/ [OR] flutter at screening (defined as ventricular response rate = 100 bpm)\n Subject with a bleeding disorder\n Subject with a history of prostatectomy because of prostate cancer, including nerve sparing techniques. Subjects with a history of surgical procedures for the treatment of benign prostate hypertrophy are permitted, [AND] with the exception of cryosurgery, [OR] cryotherapy [OR] or cryoablation\n Subject with hereditary degenerative retinal disorders such as retinitis pigmentosa\n Subject with a history of loss of vision because of [NOT] non-arteritic anterior ischemic optic neuropathy (NAION), history of temporary [OR] or permanent loss of vision, including unilateral loss of vision\n Subject with a history of congenital QT prolongation\n Subject with a penile anatomical abnormality (e.g., penile fibrosis, [OR] fractures, [OR] or Peyronie\'s disease) which, in the investigator\'s opinion, could significantly [NOT] impair sexual performance. This will be based on subject\'s reported medical history (penile exam [NOT] not required)\n Subject with primary hypoactive sexual desire.\n Subject with a spinal cord injury\n Subject with a severe chronic [OR] or acute liver disease, history of moderate (Child-Pugh B), [OR] or severe (Child-Pugh C) hepatic impairment\n Subject with clinically significant chronic hematological disease which could lead to priapism such as sickle cell anemia, [OR] multiple myeloma, [OR] and leukemia\n Subject with active peptic ulceration\n Subject with a history of malignancy within the past 5 years (other than squamous [OR] or basal cell skin cancer)\n Subject with a history of a positive test for Hepatitis B surface antigen (HbsAg) [OR] or Hepatitis C\n Subject with a known hypersensitivity to any component of the investigational medications, [OR] monoamine oxidase inhibitors, [OR] phosphodiesterase type 5 inhibitors [OR] or phenylethylamines\n Subjects with a history of drug [OR] or alcohol abuse within the past 6 months\n Subjects currently consuming =5 units of alcohol per day\n Subject who is illiterate [OR] or unable to understand the Informed Consent Form, questionnaires or subject diary\n Subject who, in the opinion of the investigator, will be [NOT] noncompliant with the visit schedule or study procedures\n Subject with any unstable medical, [OR] psychiatric, [OR] or substance abuse disorder that in the opinion of the investigator is likely to affect the subject\'s ability to complete the study [OR] or [NOT] preclude the subject\'s participation in the study\nDiagnosis of any other neurologic disease\n Uncontrolled Diabetes (Hemoglobin A1C > 7.5)<|eot_id|>']
Response: Inclusion Criteria:
Diagnosis of idiopathic Parkinson's disease that is optimally treated (motor fluctuations <20% of subject's awake time). Subjects may be on levodopa therapy [AND] but must be stable at the time of entry into the study
Sexually active (i.e. =1 attempt/week) males, 40 - 64 years of age (inclusive) at time of screening
Diagnosis of moderate erectile dysfunction (defined according to the NIH Consensus Development Panel on Impotence) for more than 6 months [AND] and demonstrating and incomplete response to tadalafil alone
Subject demonstrating an IIEF-5 drug-free baseline score that is = 10 [AND] but = 16, and an IIEF-5 tadalafil-alone baseline score that is = 18
Subject in a stable heterosexual relationship for at least 6 months. (2)
Subject motivated to seek treatment for erectile dysfunction.
Subject with a total serum testosterone level = 300 ng/dL, [AND] with or without supplementation
Hoehn and Yahr Scale score of 1 - 3
Patient able to consent and comply with protocol requirements

Exclusion Criteria:
Subject unwilling to cease use of any treatment for erectile dysfunction during the study, including oral medication, [OR] vacuum devices, [OR] constrictive devices, [OR] injections, [OR] urethral suppositories, [OR] gels, any over-the-counter [OR] or nonprescription medications, and products purchased via the internet
Subject receiving dopamine agonists, [OR] nitrates, [OR] alpha-receptor blocking agents, [OR] or antihypertensive medication (see other exclusionary medications listed below)
 Subject with a history of syncope within the last 6 months prior to screening
Subject with symptomatic postural hypotension (severe dizziness [OR] or fainting
Subject with hypotension [AND] and a resting systolic blood pressure of < 90 mmHG [OR] or hypertension [AND] with a resting systolic blood pressure > 170 mmHG [OR] or a resting diastolic blood pressure > 110 mmHG
 Subject with any underlying cardiovascular condition, including unstable angina pectoris, which [NOT] preclude sexual activity
 Subject with a history of myocardial infarction, [OR] stroke [OR] or life-threatening arrhythmia within 6 months prior to screening
 Subject with uncontrolled atrial fibrillation/ [OR] flutter at screening (defined as ventricular response rate = 100 bpm)
 Subject with a bleeding disorder
 Subject with a history of prostatectomy because of prostate cancer, including nerve sparing techniques. Subjects with a history of surgical procedures for the treatment of benign prostate hypertrophy are permitted, [AND] with the exception of cryosurgery, [OR] cryotherapy [OR] or cryoablation
 Subject with hereditary degenerative retinal disorders such as retinitis pigmentosa
 Subject with a history of loss of vision because of [NOT] non-arteritic anterior ischemic optic neuropathy (NAION), history of temporary [OR] or permanent loss of vision, including unilateral loss of vision
 Subject with a history of congenital QT prolongation
 Subject with a penile anatomical abnormality (e.g., penile fibrosis, [OR] fractures, [OR] or Peyronie's disease) which, in the investigator's opinion, could significantly [NOT] impair sexual performance. This will be based on subject's reported medical history (penile exam [NOT] not required)
 Subject with primary hypoactive sexual desire.
 Subject with a spinal cord injury
 Subject with a severe chronic [OR] or acute liver disease, history of moderate (Child-Pugh B), [OR] or severe (Child-Pugh C) hepatic impairment
 Subject with clinically significant chronic hematological disease which could lead to priapism such as sickle cell anemia, [OR] multiple myeloma, [OR] and leukemia
 Subject with active peptic ulceration
 Subject with a history of malignancy within the past 5 years (other than squamous [OR] or basal cell skin cancer)
 Subject with a history of a positive test for Hepatitis B surface antigen (HbsAg) [OR] or Hepatitis C
 Subject with a known hypersensitivity to any component of the investigational medications, [OR] monoamine oxidase inhibitors, [OR] phosphodiesterase type 5 inhibitors [OR] or phenylethylamines
 Subjects with a history of drug [OR] or alcohol abuse within the past 6 months
 Subjects currently consuming =5 units of alcohol per day
 Subject who is illiterate [OR] or unable to understand the Informed Consent Form, questionnaires or subject diary
 Subject who, in the opinion of the investigator, will be [NOT] noncompliant with the visit schedule or study procedures
 Subject with any unstable medical, [OR] psychiatric, [OR] or substance abuse disorder that in the opinion of the investigator is likely to affect the subject's ability to complete the study [OR] or [NOT] preclude the subject's participation in the study
Diagnosis of any other neurologic disease
 Uncontrolled Diabetes (Hemoglobin A1C > 7.5)
