Output: ['<|begin_of_text|>Below is an instruction that describes a task, paired with an input that provides further context. Write a response that appropriately completes the request.\n\n### Instruction:\nCreate an edited version of the following inclusion and exclusion criteria that incorporates the logical operators [AND], [OR], [NOT].\nApply these rules for inserting the logical operators:\n- Always write an [OR] before an "or", "and/or", "and / or" in the text.\n- Always write an [OR] after a comma "," and after "/" in the text.\n- Always write an [AND] before words like "with", "who", "in addition", "plus", "and", "but", "that", "despite", "having".\n- Always write a [NOT] before "no", "not", "none",  "don\'t", "free", "prevent", "Inability", "lack", "impossible", "off", "without", "unable", "naive", "excluded", "absence"\n\nGeneral rules:\n- The operators [OR], [AND], [NOT] should be written before the words like or, and, not.\n- An [AND], [OR], [NOT] must not come before a line segment (\\n).\n- Please carefully read through the given criteria text, then output only an edited version with the logical operators incorporated, enclosed in <EDITED_CRITERIA> tags.\n- Do not include any other explanations or output.\n\n\n### Input:\nInclusion Criteria:\nPatients aged =50 years with DM2 and symptomatic PAD diagnosed clinically (according to Fontaine criteria, stage IIa or IIb and III) and by measuring the <U+0391><U+0392><U+0399>.\n\nExclusion Criteria:\nCongestive heart failure, history of ventricular tachycardia, ventricular fibrillation or multifocal ventricular extrasystoles or QTc prolongation.\nPatients with atrial fibrillation taking any anticoagulant therapy or patients with a history of cardioembolic ischemic stroke or hemorrhagic stroke.\nPatients with a history (= 12 months) of acute coronary syndrome receiving dual antiplatelet therapy, or patients receiving monotherapy with aspirin.\nPatients with hepatic impairment (child-Pugh staging, calibration = 5) or renal impairment (creatinine clearance = 30ml / min), recent peptic ulcer, a history of hypersensitivity to cilostazol, cancer patients undergoing treatment.\n\n\n### Response:\nInclusion Criteria:\nPatients aged =50 years [AND] with DM2 [AND] and symptomatic PAD diagnosed clinically (according to Fontaine criteria, stage IIa [OR] or IIb [OR] and III) [AND] and by measuring the <U+0391><U+0392><U+0399>.\n\nExclusion Criteria:\nCongestive heart failure, history of ventricular tachycardia, [OR] ventricular fibrillation [OR] or multifocal ventricular extrasystoles [OR] or QTc prolongation.\nPatients with atrial fibrillation taking any anticoagulant therapy [OR] or patients with a history of cardioembolic ischemic stroke [OR] or hemorrhagic stroke.\nPatients with a history (= 12 months) of acute coronary syndrome receiving dual antiplatelet therapy, [OR] or patients receiving monotherapy with aspirin.\nPatients with hepatic impairment (child-Pugh staging, calibration = 5) [OR] or renal impairment (creatinine clearance = 30ml / min), recent peptic ulcer, a history of hypersensitivity to cilostazol, [OR] cancer patients undergoing treatment.<|eot_id|>']
Response: Inclusion Criteria:
Patients aged =50 years [AND] with DM2 [AND] and symptomatic PAD diagnosed clinically (according to Fontaine criteria, stage IIa [OR] or IIb [OR] and III) [AND] and by measuring the <U+0391><U+0392><U+0399>.

Exclusion Criteria:
Congestive heart failure, history of ventricular tachycardia, [OR] ventricular fibrillation [OR] or multifocal ventricular extrasystoles [OR] or QTc prolongation.
Patients with atrial fibrillation taking any anticoagulant therapy [OR] or patients with a history of cardioembolic ischemic stroke [OR] or hemorrhagic stroke.
Patients with a history (= 12 months) of acute coronary syndrome receiving dual antiplatelet therapy, [OR] or patients receiving monotherapy with aspirin.
Patients with hepatic impairment (child-Pugh staging, calibration = 5) [OR] or renal impairment (creatinine clearance = 30ml / min), recent peptic ulcer, a history of hypersensitivity to cilostazol, [OR] cancer patients undergoing treatment.
