rioperative intravenous (IV) steroids.\n\n\n### Response:\nInclusion Criteria:\nPatients undergoing surgeries in the upper limb (arm, [OR] forearm [OR] or hand)\n\nExclusion Criteria:\nCoagulopathies (with prothrombin concentration less than 60% [OR] or INR more than 1.5)\nIn-ability to postpone anti-coagulation medications.\nInfection [OR] or injury [OR] or a lesion at the block site.\nSuspected cervical vertebral column injury necessitating using a neck collar.\nA compromised lung on the contralateral side of the block (Pneumothorax, [OR] hemothorax [OR] or Pneumonectomy).\nTraumatic vascular injuries [OR] or operative interventions (Surgical harvesting) involving arteries of the upper limb on the operative side.\nPatients with communication difficulties.\nHypersensitivity to local anesthetics [OR] and/or Dexamethasone.\nPatients on perioperative intravenous (IV) steroids.<|eot_id|>']
Response: Inclusion Criteria:
Patients undergoing surgeries in the upper limb (arm, [OR] forearm [OR] or hand)

Exclusion Criteria:
Coagulopathies (with prothrombin concentration less than 60% [OR] or INR more than 1.5)
In-ability to postpone anti-coagulation medications.
Infection [OR] or injury [OR] or a lesion at the block site.
Suspected cervical vertebral column injury necessitating using a neck collar.
A compromised lung on the contralateral side of the block (Pneumothorax, [OR] hemothorax [OR] or Pneumonectomy).
Traumatic vascular injuries [OR] or operative interventions (Surgical harvesting) involving arteries of the upper limb on the operative side.
Patients with communication difficulties.
Hypersensitivity to local anesthetics [OR] and/or Dexamethasone.
Patients on perioperative intravenous (IV) steroids.
